  <p class="lede">Research into user interfaces where everything a person can do is a <em>claim</em>
    with a durable id, and every claim carries machine-checked evidence.</p>

  {{{ $utils.latest() }}}

  <h2 id="the-idea">The idea</h2>
  <p>A cairn is a stack of stones that marks a trail: placed by hand, built to last, and trusted by
    whoever comes next. Here the trails are a product's flows, and the stones are claims about them:
    “pressing <code>^12</code> clears the completed items”, “<code>^12</code>'s button is visible
    and on top whenever there's something to clear”. Each claim is backed by a Lean proof, a model
    check or a conformance run against the real app.</p>
  <p>To make those claims mean something, the code they talk about has to be the code that runs.
    So the app's state, view, layout, hit testing, focus and text editing are all pure Lean, and
    the theorems are about exactly those functions.</p>
  <figure class="diagram">
{{{ $utils.architecture() }}}
    <figcaption>One frame, from a key press to the screen.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <aside class="note"><div class="card px">
    <p class="label"><img src="/icon.svg" alt="">Not a layer on top</p>
    <p>Cairn isn't verification bolted onto an existing UI framework. It is its own framework: the
      app, its view, layout, hit testing, focus and text editing are written in Lean, and a thin
      Swift host hands over events and draws the result with Metal.</p>
    <p>Owning every layer is what lets a claim reach all the way down. Each one is bound to the code
      that actually runs, not to a model of someone else's, and the little we do have to trust,
      the host, is written down in one place.</p>
  </div></aside>

  <h2 id="what-a-claim-is">What a claim is</h2>
  <p>A claim is one statement about the app, in two halves, and both live in the app's source under
    one id. The human half says what a person does and what follows. The formal half is a Lean
    statement about the code that runs, and the proof that it holds. Here is the whole of the
    counter's Reset button:</p>

  <figure class="claim">
<pre class="px"><code><span class="kw">move</span> <span class="id">^2</span> reset (s : State) <span class="str">"Reset"</span> in <span class="id">^3</span> <span class="kw">where</span><i class="mk">1</i>
  <span class="kw">does</span> <span class="str">"Press Reset."</span><i class="mk">2</i>
  <span class="kw">sees</span> <span class="str">"The count is zero, and Reset disappears."</span>
  <span class="kw">enabled</span> := resetEnabled s<i class="mk">3</i>
  <span class="kw">on</span> press
    <span class="kw">apply</span> := { s with count := 0 }<i class="mk">4</i>
    <span class="kw">post</span> s' := s'.count = 0 ∧ resetEnabled s' = false<i class="mk">5</i>
    <span class="kw">sound</span> := by intro s _; exact ⟨rfl, rfl⟩<i class="mk">6</i></code></pre>
    <ol class="callouts">
      <li>The id, <code>^2</code>, then a Lean name, a title and the place the control appears in:
        <code>^3</code>, the counter's window.</li>
      <li>The human half: what the person does and what they see. Reviewers read it, and its claim
        page shows it.</li>
      <li>When the move is available. Lean proves Reset's button is on screen exactly when this holds.</li>
      <li>What pressing it does. The app's update is assembled from its moves and nothing else.</li>
      <li>The claim, formally: afterwards the count is zero and Reset is gone.</li>
      <li>The evidence. Without a proof that the handler meets the claim, the app doesn't compile.</li>
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <p>Because the update is built only from moves, two things hold for every app, proved once in
    the framework: an action that no claim owns changes nothing and asks nothing of the host
    (<code>update_unclaimed</code>), and every effect the app does ask for comes from a move
    (<code>update_effect</code>).</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="ids-are-the-contract">Ids are the contract</h3>
  <p><code>^2</code> means Reset for as long as the counter exists. Ids are never renumbered or
    reused: a claim that goes away is retired with a reason, and its id stays issued.
    <code>claims.lock</code> records every id ever issued with a hash of its formal statement. The
    build fails when an id vanishes without being retired, comes back after it, or changes kind,
    and it warns when a statement's meaning changes, because a new meaning needs a new id. A control
    that repeats once per item carries a key: <code>^5#12</code> is <code>^5</code> for item 12.
    Ids are scoped by package: Cairn's own are <code>cairn^N</code>, and an app in its own
    repository has its own, like Yap's <code>yap^311</code>.</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="six-kinds">Six kinds</h3>
  <table class="kinds">
    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it says</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>move</td><td>Something a person can do, what follows, and the proof.</td><td>^1 Increment, ^2 Reset</td></tr>
      <tr><td>flow</td><td>A goal and a path of moves that reaches it. Lean checks every step is really answered.</td><td>^4 Count to three, ^5 Start over</td></tr>
      <tr><td>invariant</td><td>A property every move keeps, so it holds in every state the app can reach.</td><td>yap^311 A shortcut never sends (Yap)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>assume</td><td>Something the proofs rely on but can't prove. Recorded, and visible in every theorem that uses it.</td><td>^7 The host reports clicks where they happen</td></tr>
      <tr><td>place</td><td>A region of the view where moves appear.</td><td>^3 Counter window</td></tr>
      <tr><td>retire</td><td>A tombstone: the claim is gone, the id stays issued.</td><td>^6 No press left in progress: the runtime owns presses now</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 class="sub" id="how-sure-we-are">How sure we are</h3>
  <p>Every claim carries its evidence, and its claim page shows which kind. Strongest first:</p>
  <ul class="trail ladder">
    <li><b>Proved.</b> Lean's kernel checked the proof, using only the three standard axioms;
      <code>make check</code> audits every theorem.</li>
    <li><b>Conformance-checked.</b> The real app, driven through
      generated sequences of moves, agrees with the model after every step.</li>
    <li><b>Assumed.</b> Recorded, never proved. Theorems that rely on it take it as a hypothesis,
      so every use shows.</li>
    <li><b>Seen.</b> A screenshot of the real app with the claim's control pinned on it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every package on the registry gets claim pages: every claim it makes, grouped by place, with
    its proofs, generated from what it published and nothing written by hand. See Cairn's for the
    <a href="/packages/cairn/claims/3/">counter</a>, <a href="/packages/cairn/claims/106/">Basics</a>,
    <a href="/packages/cairn/claims/201/">TodoMVC</a> and the
    <a href="/packages/cairn/claims/#assumptions">trusted base</a>.</p>

